WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.890
The first trailer for the follow-up to Avengers:
Infinity War has arrived.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.090
One thing we've all been wondering is what
role, exactly, Ant-Man will play in all of

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.090
this.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.389
Let's break out our microscopes for a close
look at how things seem to be shaping up for

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.680
Ant-Man in Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.840
Infinity War already assembled a staggering
cast of characters, but Endgame will finally

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.590
mark Scott Lang's entrance to the two-part
arc.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.660
He's been busy at home, honing his drum skills
and building sweet cardboard slides with his

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.660
daughter.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.720
The end of his house arrest was marked not
by a reunion with the Avengers, but by a desperate

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.410
quest to rescue Janet van Dyne from the Quantum
Realm and stop some dangerous tech from falling

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.590
into the wrong hands.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.750
It's no wonder he was too busy to show up
for Infinity War's many battles.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.940
The end credit scene for Ant-Man and the Wasp
showed us that Lang was caught in the Quantum

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.730
Realm when half of humanity was dusted - including,
seemingly, the only people who could get him

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.110
out - making him one of the less fortunate
among the survivors.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.670
The Endgame trailer shows us Bruce Banner
looking at images of Lang, Peter Parker, and

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.660
even Shuri, all of whom were not "in plain
sight" when the snap took place.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.250
The scene seems to imply that thanks to Lang's
miniature predicament, the survivors think

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.290
he, too, disintegrated with the others.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.940
That, combined with the last scene in the
trailer, where Black Widow and Captain America

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.330
see Lang show up at the front door of the
Avengers HQ facility, would make Lang's survival

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.760
not just a happy relief, but very likely an
integral part of the plot.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.640
For a long while now, fans have theorized
that time travel will be a factor in undoing

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.500
the last moments of Infinity War.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.300
Of course, the Russo Brothers have made it
clear that the MCU hasn't built up a ten-year,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.890
20-movie universe just to have their heroes
quickly crank back the clock and save the

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.890
day.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.950
There are going to be casualties here, regardless
of what happens, but that doesn't take away

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.670
the fact that time travel could still be pivotal
in saving the day - and Scott Lang will probably

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.480
bring some newfound time travel expertise
with him.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.640
"Can you buzz me in?"

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.110
Savvy MCU fans may remember something Janet
Van Dyne told Scott near the very end of Ant-Man

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.740
and the Wasp, just before he jumped into the
Quantum Realm:

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.870
"And don't get sucked into a time vortex.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.530
We won't be able to save you."

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.920
Doctor Strange relinquished the Time Stone
to Thanos and then got himself dusted for

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:08.920
the trouble.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.450
Scarlet Witch, likewise, ceased to exist at
the end of Infinity War - and with those two

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.630
reality-warping characters off the table,
that leaves Lang to provide whatever time

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.100
travel expertise he can to the Avengers.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.700
Since he somehow managed to escape the Quantum
Realm despite the Pym family's disappearance,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.730
it stands to reason that he's got some knowledge
to drop.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.450
Another crucial part of using the Quantum
Realm for time travel?

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.450
Equipment.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.980
You don't just click your heels together and
wish you were there.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.480
It takes a massive array of specially-made
suits, fancy buttons and levers, and a lab

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.980
that - even in the back of a van - looks rather
intimidating to run.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.860
Ant-Man's got the keys to the Ant-Van, a piece
of equipment that could turn out to be just

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.040
as important in the fight against Thanos as
Thor's mighty weapon Stormbreaker.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.800
At the end of the day, no matter how bad things
get, we're talking about Ant-Man here.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.360
Paul Rudd radiates humor, and the MCU Scott
Lang persona has been crafted to accommodate

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.360
this.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.410
The results have been one of Marvel's chief
sources of unrestricted comic relief regardless

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:10.090
of the situation, which is precisely why the
Ant-Man movies have been so lighthearted.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.520
Having such an effortlessly funny character
involved in a story like Endgame may be an

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.500
even greater relief than we imagined.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.980
Infinity War was already an intense ride,
even with the Guardians' attitudes, Tony Stark's

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.780
sharp dialogue, and Peter Parker's nervous
banter helping to relieve the tension.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.349
"I'm Peter by the way.”

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:27.349
"Doctor Strange."

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:28.920
"Oh, we're using made up names.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.520
Um, I'm Spider-Man, then.”

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.160
But now, most of those characters are either
out of the picture or in no mood for quips,

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.050
and it may fall to Mr. Lang to bail out the
tone with some quick wit and humor, very likely

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.720
to the relief of us all.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.640
Finally, we have an easily-overlooked little
conclusion that supports the importance of

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:51.650
Ant-Man in Endgame - the fact that the trailer
ends with him front and center.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.700
If Lang was just another pawn on the chessboard
keeping the game in motion, Marvel probably

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.230
would have just popped in a split second shot
of him on the security camera in front of

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.459
the Avengers' HQ.

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:04.489
But instead, they not only devoted plenty
of time to his appearance, they put it at

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:07.770
the trailer's end, in a place of cinematic
honor.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.160
The end of a good trailer should serve as
a powerful, attention-grabbing cliffhanger,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.410
where a movie's marketing department drop
an idea they want to leave you pondering while

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.150
you wait for the final product.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:21.319
It's usually in these final moments that trailers
tip their hand as they try to leave a trail

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:24.860
of breadcrumbs that will guide people straight
to the theater on opening night.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:25.860
Did it work?

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:26.650
We know we'll be there.

